## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design robust, scalable schema evolution in a multi-tenant streaming system, including compatibility rules, rollouts, and testing under mixed versions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema registry
- Versioned event envelopes
- Backward/forward compatibility
- Rolling upgrades and dual writes
- Data migration and validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: coerce event to current schema version per tenant
function coerce(event, currentVersion) {
  if (event.schemaVersion === currentVersion) return event;
  if (currentVersion === 2 && event.schemaVersion === 1) {
    return { ...event, region: event.region ?? "unknown", schemaVersion: 2 };
  }
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle removing a field or changing types across tenants?
- How would you instrument metrics to detect schema drift and upgrade failures?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical schema evolution handling in KCNA beginners: safe deserialization by versioning, backward compatibility, DLQ handling, and testability with canaries.

## Key Concepts
- Schema versioning
- In-memory deserializer registry
- Backward compatibility
- Dead-letter routing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal example
type Message = { payload: any };
type Deser = (payload: any)=>any;

const registry = new Map<number, Deser>();
registry.set(1, (p)=> ({id: p.id, t: p.time}));
registry.set(2, (p)=> ({id: p.id, ts: p.time, meta: p.meta}));

function deserialize(record: { payload: any }) {
  const v = Number(record.payload?.schema_version ?? 1);
  const deser = registry.get(v) ?? registry.get(1);
  if (!deser) throw new Error('No deserializer');
  return deser(record.payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support field renames without changing consumer code?
- How would you test compatibility across v1 and v2 payloads in a canary run?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes privacy-preserving data analytics in KCNA at multi-tenant scale, combining differential privacy with optional zero-knowledge proofs, testing for collusion risks and system-level tradeoffs.

## Key Concepts
- Differential privacy per tenant with clamped counts
- Gaussian noise scaling and budget accounting
- Zero-knowledge proofs for auditability
- Collusion resistance and timing-attack considerations
- Observability and canary testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function addGaussianNoise(value, sigma) {
  const u1 = Math.random();
  const u2 = Math.random();
  const z0 = Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(u1)) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * u2);
  return value + z0 * sigma;
}
```
